const pageTitle = 'How Many Days in the Algarve? | Portugal Considered';
const pageDescription =
  'Decide how many days to spend in the Algarve, from a focused two- or three-night stay to a longer trip exploring contrasting parts of southern Portugal.';
---

<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <article class="guide">
    <header class="guide-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">PORTUGAL TRIP PLANNING</p>

      <h1>How Many Days in the Algarve?</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        How to Decide How Much Time Portugal's Southern Coast Deserves
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="guide-image guide-hero">
      <img
        src="/images/algarve-how-many-days-hero.jpg"
        alt="Golden cliffs and turquoise water along the Algarve coast in southern Portugal"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The Algarve can create a difficult decision when you're planning a
      Portugal trip.
    </p>

    <p>You may already have Lisbon.</p>

    <p>Perhaps Porto and the Douro Valley.</p>

    <p>Maybe Sintra.</p>

    <p>Possibly Évora and the Alentejo.</p>

    <p>And then there is the Algarve.</p>

    <p>
      On a map, Portugal's southern coast can look like one more region to add.
    </p>

    <p>
      But adding the Algarve isn't quite like adding another city.
    </p>

    <p>Getting there takes time.</p>

    <p>The region itself stretches across very different landscapes.</p>

    <p>
      And once you arrive, the question isn't simply whether you can see the
      Algarve.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is whether you have enough time to experience the version of the
      Algarve that made you want to go there in the first place.
    </p>

    <p>So instead of asking:</p>

    <p><strong>How many days do I need to see the Algarve?</strong></p>

    <p>Ask:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <strong>
        How much time would make the Algarve worth adding to this particular
        Portugal trip?
      </strong>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>

    <p>
      For most first-time visitors, think about the Algarve this way.
    </p>

    <h3>Two or three nights — Choose focus.</h3>

    <p>
      You can have a worthwhile Algarve experience, but don't try to cover the
      region.
    </p>

    <p>
      Choose one base and let one part of the Algarve define the trip.
    </p>

    <h3>Four nights — Choose breathing room.</h3>

    <p>
      Four nights gives the Algarve enough time to feel less like an excursion
      from the rest of Portugal.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can settle into one base, experience the coast and still have room
      for an outing without turning every day into transportation.
    </p>

    <h3>Five nights — Choose depth or variety.</h3>

    <p>This is where the decision becomes interesting.</p>

    <p>
      Stay in one place and experience that part of the Algarve more deeply.
    </p>

    <p>Or introduce a contrasting second part of the region.</p>

    <h3>Six or seven nights — Choose contrast.</h3>

    <p>
      With nearly a week, two bases can begin to make real sense—especially
      when they give you genuinely different Algarve experiences.
    </p>

    <p>The principle is:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <strong>
        More nights should create a better Algarve experience—not simply a
        longer list of places.
      </strong>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>First, Understand That There Isn't One Algarve</h2>

    <p>
      One reason the question is difficult is that travelers often talk about
      the Algarve as though it were a single destination.
    </p>

    <p>It isn't.</p>

    <p>
      The western Algarve can give you dramatic cliffs, coves, exposed
      coastline and places such as Lagos and Sagres.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central Algarve contains some of the region's greatest concentration
      of beaches, resorts, restaurants and visitor infrastructure.
    </p>

    <p>The eastern Algarve changes again.</p>

    <figure class="guide-image">
      <img
        src="/images/algarve-tavira-island-beach.jpg"
        alt="Sandy beach and coastal landscape on Tavira Island in the eastern Algarve"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Around Faro, Tavira and the Ria Formosa, barrier islands, lagoons,
      waterways and broad sandy beaches replace much of the cliff scenery
      travelers associate with the Algarve.
    </p>

    <p>
      These aren't merely different stops along the same coast.
    </p>

    <p>They can create different vacations.</p>

    <p>
      That means the number of days you need depends partly on
      <strong>which Algarve you actually want to experience.</strong>
    </p>

